$files['docs/BUILD-PLAN.md'] = @'
# Build plan — Governed MCP on Azure

Build the modules in this order, one session and one branch per module.
Do not start a module until the previous one validates.

## End-state architecture

```text
MCP clients: VS Code + GitHub Copilot, MCP Inspector (optional: Copilot Studio, Foundry agent)
 → Azure API Management (only entry point): Protected Resource Metadata (PRM), validate-azure-ad-token,
   per-tool role check, rate limit, IP filter, payload-free logs
   → Azure Functions MCP server (.NET isolated worker), built-in Entra authentication. Tools:
       get_service_health   read, synthetic data
       create_ticket        write, synthetic side effect (Storage queue)
       whoami               On-Behalf-Of → Microsoft Graph /me
       read_report_as_user  On-Behalf-Of → Azure Storage (the caller's RBAC applies)
       read_report_as_app   managed identity → Azure Storage (same result for everyone)
 → Application Insights / Log Analytics
Azure API Center registers the gateway endpoint (inventory, not a runtime hop).
```

Personas:
- **Alice**: assigned, app roles Tools.Read + Tools.Write, Storage Blob Data Reader.
- **Bob**: assigned, Tools.Read only, no storage role.
- **Eve**: not assigned.

## 00 — Prerequisites and environment
- **Take:** Azure CLI, Terraform, PowerShell 7, .NET 10 and Functions Core Tools >= 4.5.0 from the pinned sample;
  Node.js >= 22.19.0 for the selected MCP Inspector. See [source selections](sources.md) before pinning exact tool versions.
- **Build:** .devcontainer; preflight.ps1 (tools, versions, `az login`, subscription, resource providers, role checks,
  quota, region); docs/requests/identity-request.md listing exactly what an identity administrator must create or consent.
- **Show:** preflight passes; the identity request is clear enough to send as is.
- **Implementation now available:** EN/FR module 00 guide, read-only explicit-context preflight, administrator
  identity request, pinned workstation configuration and private stable participant parameters.
  The shared root supports the group foundation and a default-off public B1 App Service.
  Offline schema/mock/script/server evidence is separate from container and fresh-subscription validation,
  which remain pending. No MCP runtime or Entra root has been implemented.

## 01 — Discover MCP (no Azure)
- **Take:** MCP specification: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification (revision 2026-07-28 verified on 2026-10-14: architecture, tools, transports, authorization);
  https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/mcp-servers; MCP Inspector (@modelcontextprotocol/inspector).
- **Build:** a local MCP server from the official C# SDK quickstart (https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk);
  connect it to VS Code and to MCP Inspector.
- **Show:** tools/list, a tool call, the approval prompt in the client.
- **Validate:** Inspector lists the tools and returns a result. Check the negotiated protocol version: the current
  specification uses per-request metadata, while older clients use an initialization handshake. Do not mix message shapes.

## 02 — An "open" MCP server (level 0)
- **Take:** https://github.com/Azure-Samples/remote-mcp-functions-dotnet
  at `468c518c884ec318e3001b9573254c9e775409c9`;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/scenario-custom-remote-mcp-server;
  Functions MCP extension docs; Flex Consumption docs.
- **Build:** Terraform for Function App (Flex Consumption), Storage, Application Insights, managed identity;
  the five tools with synthetic data. Use a queue for synthetic tickets. In this module, downstream identity tools
  return clearly labeled synthetic demonstrations; their real authorization behavior is introduced in module 05.
- **Show:** with the key, anyone can call create_ticket.
- **Validate:** HTTP 401 without the MCP extension system key, HTTP 200 with the key and a valid MCP request;
  anonymous access disabled. Set webhook authorization to `System`: upstream `host.json` uses `Anonymous` because
  the upstream sample starts with Entra authentication. Never copy that setting into the key-only module.

## 03 — Identity with Microsoft Entra (level 1)
- **Take:** https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/app-service/configure-authentication-mcp;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/app-service/configure-authentication-mcp-server-vscode;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-mcp-tutorial;
  app roles: https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/identity-platform/howto-add-app-roles-in-apps.
- **Build:** infra/entra: app registration (Application ID URI, scope user_impersonation, app roles Tools.Read,
  Tools.Write, Tools.Execute.All), assignment required, pre-authorized client applications (VS Code client ID from
  the sample, Azure CLI); enable built-in authentication and Protected Resource Metadata (PRM) (setting
  WEBSITE_AUTH_PRM_DEFAULT_WITH_SCOPES, Preview).
- **Show:** VS Code signs in; explain audience (`aud`), scopes (`scp`), and roles using synthetic claim examples;
  Eve is refused. Never paste a live token into a website, chat, committed file, or log.
- **Validate:** 401 without token; 401 with a token for another audience; 200 for Alice and Bob;
  Eve cannot get a token (assignment required).

## 04 — API Management as the only entry point (level 2)
- **Take:** https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/expose-existing-mcp-server;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/secure-mcp-servers;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/mcp-server-overview (governance, limitations);
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/manage-mcp-servers-rest-api (REST, Bicep, Azure CLI, Terraform);
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/validate-azure-ad-token-policy;
  https://github.com/Azure-Samples/AI-Gateway (labs mcp-client-authorization, mcp-prm-oauth).
- **Build:** APIM v2 (azapi, api-version 2025-09-01-preview), MCP server exposed; policies: validate-azure-ad-token
  (audience = the MCP server), role check per tool from the JSON-RPC body (create_ticket requires Tools.Write),
  rate-limit-by-key on the caller object ID, ip-filter; Application Insights with zero body bytes;
  Function reachable only through APIM (choose and document the network option in the plan step).
- **Recommendation:** Standard v2 outbound virtual network integration, a Function private endpoint, private DNS,
  and public Function access disabled. The MCP management API is **Preview**. Keep public protected resource metadata
  and OAuth challenges on the gateway. Do not expose the private Function URL to clients.
- **Show:** Bob calls create_ticket → 403, Alice → 200; burst → 429; direct call to the Function refused; logs without payload.
- **Validate:** all of the above as PASS/FAIL lines.
- **Optional 04b:** expose a synthetic REST API as an MCP server (https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/export-rest-mcp-server; AI-Gateway lab mcp-from-api).

## 05 — Who carries the rights? On-Behalf-Of vs app-only (level 3)
- **Take:** https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/identity-platform/v2-oauth2-on-behalf-of-flow;
  src/FunctionsMcpTool/HelloToolWithAuth.cs in the sample; federated identity credential with a user-assigned managed identity
  (https://learn.microsoft.com/entra/workload-id/workload-identity-federation-config-app-trust-managed-identity).
- **Build:** whoami and read_report_as_user (On-Behalf-Of, Graph User.Read and Azure Storage user_impersonation);
  read_report_as_app (managed identity, Storage Blob Data Reader on one container).
- **Show:** same question, Alice vs Bob: as_user → 200 for Alice, 403 for Bob; as_app → same result for both.
- **Validate:** the four results; no client secret anywhere. Record both outer HTTP status and the MCP tool result:
  a downstream HTTP 403 can be returned as a tool error inside an HTTP 200 response.

## 06 — Observe and inventory (level 4)
- **Take:** APIM Application Insights integration and KQL; Azure API Center MCP inventory:
  https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-center/register-discover-mcp-server;
  AI-Gateway lab mcp-registry-apic;
  bring your own (BYO) MCP server in the Microsoft 365 admin center (Preview): https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/admin/manage/manage-byo-mcp-server.
- **Build:** a workbook or saved KQL queries (tool, caller, status, latency, top denials); register the server in API Center.
- **Show:** who called which tool, when, with what result.
- **Optional 06b (Preview):** Agent 365 / Microsoft 365 admin center inventory as a demo only (Preview, licensing and
  Frontier requirements), no participant step. Source: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/agent-365
  (Preview, verified 2026-10-14) and the
  [Tech Community post](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/integrationsonazureblog/microsoft-agent-365-and-azure-api-management-integration-for-mcp-governance/4563335).
  Facts to respect:
  - Needs an Agent 365 tenant and a tenant administrator's one-time consent that grants the **A365 MCP Gateway Policy
    Operator** role at tenant level, with read and write on API-scoped policies. This widens a role beyond one
    resource group, so it is never a participant step (security invariant 8).
  - With API Management v2 (our core gateway): inventory and block/unblock of a server only (blocked server returns 403).
    Tool-level blocking, tool activity observability and Defender/Purview threat protection need the **AI Gateway tier
    (Preview)**, which stays outside the core path.
  - Tool-level control needs a static list of allowed tools on each MCP server.
  - Agent 365 does not route agents through the gateway: the client must use the gateway endpoint, and direct access to
    the MCP server must stay blocked.

## 07 — Optional: other clients
- **Take:** https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/mcp-authentication;
  https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-copilot-studio/mcp-add-existing-server-to-agent.
- **Show:** the same governed server from a Foundry agent (project managed identity or OAuth identity passthrough, Preview)
  and from Copilot Studio. Facilitator demo only.

## 99 — Clean up
- terraform destroy for workload and entra; delete role assignments and test users;
  check that nothing remains (`az resource list --tag workshop=...`).

## Docs to produce
- docs/concepts/: token chain diagram, scopes vs app roles, On-Behalf-Of vs managed identity, MCP threat model
  (tool poisoning, prompt injection, token passthrough, confused deputy, over-broad tools).
- docs/facilitator-guide.md: timings, expected outputs, fallback recordings, common errors.
- docs/sources.md: every link above with its last verified date.

## P1 recommendations (design only)

Last source review: 2026-10-14. These are implementation recommendations, not a deployed or validated workshop.
The selected upstream files, versions, licenses, updates, and exclusions are in [sources.md](sources.md).

### Target repository tree

The scaffold, board, workstation preparation and resource-group foundation are present now. The following is the
complete intended tree; runtime modules still need implementation. Every module has the same README contract and its own
`validate.ps1`, `validate.sh`, `cleanup.ps1`, and `cleanup.sh`. Shared Terraform lives in two roots, not one
copy per module. Module folders contain only module-specific assets and instructions.

```text
.devcontainer/
  devcontainer.json
  Dockerfile
.github/
  agents/workshop-builder.agent.md
  copilot-instructions.md
  skills/workshop-module-builder/SKILL.md
  extensions/workshop-build-board/
    extension.mjs, progress.mjs, journey.mjs, server.mjs, board.html, progress.test.mjs
.vscode/mcp.json
.mcp.json
docs/
  BUILD-PLAN.md, PROGRESS.md, COPILOT-RUNBOOK.md, PRACTICE.md, sources.md
  requests/identity-request.md
  concepts/token-chain.md
  concepts/scopes-and-app-roles.md
  concepts/obo-and-managed-identity.md
  concepts/mcp-threat-model.md
  facilitator-guide.md
infra/
  entra/
    versions.tf, providers.tf, main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf
    .terraform.lock.hcl
  workload/
    versions.tf, providers.tf, main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf
    network.tf, gateway.tf, diagnostics.tf, inventory.tf
    .terraform.lock.hcl
src/
  LocalMcpServer/
    LocalMcpServer.csproj, Program.cs, Tools.cs
  FunctionsMcpServer/
    FunctionsMcpServer.csproj, Program.cs, host.json
    Tools/ServiceHealthTool.cs, Tools/CreateTicketTool.cs
    Tools/WhoAmITool.cs, Tools/ReadReportAsUserTool.cs, Tools/ReadReportAsAppTool.cs
    Identity/DownstreamCredentials.cs
tests/
  FunctionsMcpServer.Tests/
scripts/
  preflight.ps1, preflight.sh
  deploy.ps1, deploy.sh
  lib/Validation.psm1, lib/validation.sh
modules/
  00-prerequisites/             README, validation, cleanup
  01-discover-mcp/              README, validation, cleanup, client-config/
  02-open-server/               README, validation, cleanup, synthetic-data/
  03-entra-authentication/      README, validation, cleanup, synthetic-claims/
  04-governed-gateway/          README, validation, cleanup, policies/
  05-delegated-and-app-only/    README, validation, cleanup, synthetic-data/
  06-observe-and-inventory/    README, validation, cleanup, queries/
  07-other-clients/            README, validation, cleanup, demo-checklist.md
  99-cleanup/                  README, validation, cleanup
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md          Add when upstream code is first adapted
README.md, LICENSE, AGENTS.md, setup-workshop-mcp.ps1
```

### Choices and implementation gates

| Area | Recommendation | Gate before implementation or deployment |
|---|---|---|
| Runtime | .NET 10 isolated worker; Functions host v4; ordinary MCP tool trigger extension 1.5.0 as the upstream starting point | Confirm deployed runtime support, restore and test the minimal tool project. Do not import the sample's Apps/QR/preview SDK dependencies unnecessarily. |
| Local introduction | C# SDK 2.2.0, minimal stdio tool, synthetic arguments | Pin its package and hosting dependencies; send logs to stderr. No weather API or model subscription needed. |
| Inspector | GitHub 2.11.0 source candidate; practice launch pins the registry-available 2.9.0, Node.js >= 22.19.0 | Launch testing found no 2.11.0 package in the configured npm registry. Check availability before launch; test both local and gateway connections. Learn still recommends 0.9.0 in older APIM tips; do not adopt it blindly. Keep tokens in memory or an OS keychain, not plaintext container storage. |
| Protocol | Teach current 2026-07-28 terminology and explicitly explain legacy negotiation | Verify what each pinned client/server actually supports. APIM documents a minimum of 2025-06-18, not proof of every 2026 feature. |
| Terraform | Candidate exact pins: azurerm 5.9.0, azuread 3.10.0, azapi 2.13.0 | Versions exist in the registry; compatibility is not deployment-tested. Commit lock files after module validation, with no state/tfvars. |
| Resource model | AzureRM for Flex, storage, identities, private networking, and supported gateway settings; AzAPI for missing properties and MCP APIs/policies at `2025-09-01-preview` (**Preview**) | Use `Microsoft.ApiManagement/service/apis` with `type: mcp`, `transportType: streamable` and the real Function endpoint `/runtime/webhooks/mcp`. Existing-server proxying does not need REST tool subresources. |
| Network | Standard v2 public gateway with outbound virtual network integration to a Function private endpoint; private DNS and public Function access disabled | Dedicated delegated APIM subnet with network security group; separate private endpoint subnet. Prove gateway access and public direct denial; decide how deployment reaches restricted services. Basic v2 lacks this outbound integration. |
| Region | Evaluate West Europe as the first candidate, with no assumed availability | Preflight must check current Flex locations/runtime, Standard v2 availability, regional quota and policy restrictions for the chosen subscription. Ask before using a different region. |
| Identity | One MCP audience, user scope plus Tools.Read/Tools.Write app roles; assignment required | Identity administrator approves exact objects, consent, test users, and preauthorized clients. Read existing IDs when creation is skipped. |
| Token boundary | Gateway and Function protect one logical MCP resource; the client token is validated there, never sent to Graph or Storage | Separate downstream tokens via On-Behalf-Of (OBO) or managed identity. If gateway and backend become distinct audiences, design explicit token exchange; do not broaden the Function's accepted audiences. |
| OBO | User-assigned managed identity plus a federated identity credential, supported Azure Identity libraries | Downstream Graph User.Read and Storage delegated consent; same-tenant application/identity trust. Reject app-only calls to user-context tools. Never fall back to developer identity in deployed validation. |
| App-only | Separate explicit managed-identity credential for the app-context tool | Storage Blob Data Reader on the one synthetic report container, not the entire subscription. |
| Authorization | Platform token validation plus per-tool role checks; no automatic approval for writes | Preserve the request body when inspecting JSON-RPC. Deny malformed or unsupported call shapes safely. Verify gateway HTTP 403 and distinguish downstream errors embedded in MCP HTTP 200. |
| Telemetry | Authenticated caller object ID, fixed tool name, status, latency and correlation ID only | Zero body bytes on frontend/backend requests/responses at all inherited scopes. Never read streaming response bodies, log arguments/results, or trust caller-supplied identity headers. |
| Inventory | Register only the governed gateway endpoint in API Center | Do not import a third-party server catalog. Inventory registration does not itself authorize runtime access. |

### Risks, permissions, costs, and cleanup

- **Identity permissions:** separate Azure resource creation from Microsoft Entra application/consent work.
  A workload participant needs resource deployment permissions on the workshop resource group. The identity
  administrator needs application management and the consent permissions required by the selected delegated
  APIs. Role assignment permissions are separate from Contributor. Do not request Owner by default.
- **Test identities:** Alice and Bob need actual assigned app roles; Eve remains unassigned. Use dedicated
  non-production test accounts. `whoami` must return an allowlisted synthetic persona, not a live display name
  or email copied into a workshop artifact.
- **Preview:** App Service protected resource metadata, the APIM MCP management API, the canvas SDK surface,
  and optional Microsoft 365 BYO inventory remain explicitly labeled. Self-hosted MCP on Functions is
  **Preview** and is not selected. Recheck optional client feature status before its demo.
- **Source conflicts:** the App Service VS Code walkthrough auto-creates a client secret; use the sample's
  managed-identity federation pattern instead. PRM lab text has an RFC number typo and older authorization
  language; the standard is RFC 9728. API Management overview and existing-server docs disagree on resources;
  use only tools in the core workshop. Do not copy OAuth-server emulation or permissive multi-audience examples.
- **Costs:** Standard v2 is provisioned gateway capacity, not a few Functions executions. Include private
  endpoint, storage, telemetry ingestion/retention, API Center and optional agent charges in the estimate
  before approval. Keep always-ready Functions instances off unless required. No prices are assumed here.
- **Cleanup:** module 99 destroys workload first, then only workshop-created Entra objects and test accounts.
  Preserve provided identities and existing infrastructure. Check role assignments, private DNS/endpoint
  resources, API Center records, soft-deletion/retention behavior, and the complete workshop-tagged inventory.
- **Completion:** prove the previous module first; never convert a design recommendation or canvas save into
  validation evidence. A private gateway design is not implemented until a fresh deployment proves it.

## Sources

See [sources.md](sources.md) for the source register, exact upstream pins, reuse decisions, and limitations.
Last verified: 2026-10-14.
'@

$files['docs/COPILOT-RUNBOOK.md'] = @'
# Runbook — build this workshop with the GitHub Copilot app

This runbook drives the build from this scaffold to a validated workshop: one module per session, one branch and one pull request per module.

Participants do not need this authoring workflow. Start with [workspace choice](GETTING-STARTED.md):
VS Code dev container or local terminal, common tool checks, private participant suffix and the Terraform group foundation.
The board opens `#/start` by default. No module is validated by choosing an environment.

## 1. Before you start

- **GitHub Copilot app** installed and signed in. With Copilot Business or Enterprise, your organization must allow it.
- **Local tools:** Git, GitHub CLI, Azure CLI, Terraform, PowerShell 7, Node.js LTS, and the .NET SDK and Azure Functions Core Tools versions used by
  [Azure-Samples/remote-mcp-functions-dotnet](https://github.com/Azure-Samples/remote-mcp-functions-dotnet).
- **A non-production Azure subscription** where you can create resources and role assignments:
  `az login` then `az account set --subscription <your-test-subscription>`.
- **Test identities:** a test Microsoft Entra tenant, or an identity administrator who can run `infra/entra`
  and create three test users: Alice, Bob and Eve.
- **Recommended — Microsoft Learn MCP server**, so the agent reads the official documentation before writing code:
  - GitHub Copilot app or Copilot CLI: add it from the Customize panel or with `/mcp add`
    (name `microsoft-learn`, type HTTP, URL `https://learn.microsoft.com/api/mcp`, no authentication).
  - Workspace: already configured in portable `.mcp.json`, with `.vscode/mcp.json` for compatibility.
    Both describe the same public endpoint, without credentials. Avoid adding a duplicate server in the same client.

### Bootstrap an empty destination

From the folder containing `setup-workshop-mcp.ps1`, run PowerShell 7:

```powershell
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\setup-workshop-mcp.ps1
```

The standalone bootstrap includes the scaffold, current P1 source assessment, and build board. It requires Git
and GitHub CLI (`gh`) unless `-SkipGitHub` is used. If missing, install them with
`winget install --id Git.Git -e` and `winget install --id GitHub.cli -e`, then reopen the terminal.
Git identity must be configured. For publication, the script uses repository-local GitHub noreply identity,
not the globally configured email. It does not change global Git settings.

The default destination is `C:\github\workshop-mcp`. The script refuses a nonempty destination or an existing
remote repository. Do not empty a working folder to bypass this protection.
Options: `-Visibility private`, `-SkipGitHub` (local only), `-OpenVSCode`, `-Path`, and `-RepoName`.
The script does not create or change Azure or Microsoft Entra resources.

### Start from an existing scaffold

If the workshop files are already present, open that folder in a local Copilot session. Do not run the bootstrap
against it. Initialize Git on the existing files, review the publication content, and then create/push the remote
repository only after explicit approval. Use a repository-local noreply Git identity and inspect the staged diff
before the first commit. Keep publication audit terms and local scan reports outside the repository.

The bootstrap contains embedded copies of the scaffold. When updating these files, update its embedded copies
in the same change, and verify a `-SkipGitHub` run in a new, empty test destination. Never test publication by
creating an extra public repository.

## 2. How the build works

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | Permanent rules: content, nine security invariants, stack, module contract |
| `docs/BUILD-PLAN.md` | What to take, in which order, what each module must show and validate |
| `docs/PROGRESS.md` | Status of each module, validation evidence, open decisions |
| `docs/sources.md` | Verified upstream snapshots, useful elements, updates, exclusions and license boundaries |
| `docs/PRACTICE.md` | Client launch recipes, call expectations, per-module hints and safety implications |
| `.github/extensions/workshop-build-board/` | Read-only workshop SPA (**Preview** canvas SDK), backed by repository guides and build progress |
| `.github/skills/workshop-module-builder/SKILL.md` | The procedure the agent follows for every module |
| `.github/agents/workshop-builder.agent.md` | Optional custom agent that applies the skill |
| `AGENTS.md` | Entry point for any coding agent |

Rules of the game:
- One module = one session = one branch = one pull request. Merge it before you start the next module.
- Use **Plan** mode to think, **Interactive** mode for anything that touches Azure or Microsoft Entra,
  **Autopilot** only for documentation.
- Use **local** sessions for modules that deploy to Azure: they need your `az login`.
- The agent must show you the Terraform plan and the Entra changes, and wait for your approval, before applying anything.

## 3. Session sequence

| Step | Mode | Session | Prompt | Done when |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Plan | Local | P1 | You approved the tree, the technical choices and the risks |
| 2 | Interactive | Local | P2 | preflight.ps1 and the module 01 validate script pass |
| 3 | Interactive | Local | P3 | Module 02 validate passes |
| 4 | Interactive | Local | P4 | Module 03 validate passes |
| 5 | Interactive | Local | P5 | Module 04 validate passes |
| 6 | Interactive | Local | P6 | Module 05 validate passes |
| 7 | Interactive | Local | P7 | Module 06 validate passes |
| 8 | Interactive | Local | P8 | Module 99 leaves the subscription empty |
| D | Autopilot | Cloud or local, in parallel from step 3 | PD | Concept docs and facilitator guide merged |
| 9 | Plan | Local | P9 | Final review pull request open, no blocking gap left |

### P1 — Plan the whole build (no code)

```text
Read .github/copilot-instructions.md, docs/BUILD-PLAN.md and docs/PROGRESS.md, then the official pages listed in the plan.
Propose the full repository tree, the files of each module, the technical choices (network path from API Management
to the Function, Terraform providers and azapi resources, region) and the risks (Entra permissions, preview features,
cost). Do not write code. Record your recommendations under "Open decisions" in docs/PROGRESS.md.
```

### P2 — Modules 00 and 01

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill. Build module 00 (.devcontainer, preflight.ps1, docs/requests/identity-request.md),
and stop when its validation passes. Update docs/PROGRESS.md and open the pull request.
After merge, start a separate session/branch for module 01 and stop when its validate script passes.
```

### P3 — Module 02, the "open" server

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 02. Participant prefix p00, my current Azure subscription.
Show me the Terraform plan before applying. Fix until validate.ps1 passes.
```

### P4 — Module 03, Microsoft Entra

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 03. Generate infra/entra first and list exactly which Entra objects,
permissions and consents it creates; apply only after my approval. Then enable built-in authentication and the
Protected Resource Metadata on the Function, and prove the expected results for Alice, Bob and Eve.
```

### P5 — Module 04, API Management as the only entry point

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 04. API Management must become the only entry point.
Prove with validate.ps1: Bob gets 403 on create_ticket, Alice gets 200, 429 after the limit,
a direct call to the Function is refused, and the logs show tool name and caller without any payload.
```

### P6 — Module 05, On-Behalf-Of vs app-only

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 05. Compare On-Behalf-Of and app-only access with Alice and Bob.
validate.ps1 must check the four expected results automatically. No client secret anywhere.
```

### P7 — Module 06, observe and inventory

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 06: saved KQL queries or a workbook (tool, caller, status, latency,
top denials) and the registration of the server in Azure API Center.
```

### P8 — Module 99, clean up

```text
Use the workshop-module-builder skill for module 99. Destroy workload and entra, remove role assignments and test users,
and prove that nothing tagged with the workshop remains.
```

### PD — Documentation (parallel session, Autopilot)

```text
Write docs/concepts/ (token chain diagram, scopes vs app roles, On-Behalf-Of vs managed identity, MCP threat model),
docs/facilitator-guide.md and docs/sources.md from docs/BUILD-PLAN.md and the modules already validated.
Do not change any module code or infrastructure. Open a pull request.
```

### P9 — Final review

```text
Review the whole repository against .github/copilot-instructions.md: the nine security invariants, no customer or
internal content, every link valid, Preview labels present, every module cleans up, and module 99 leaves a fresh
subscription empty. List every gap in a pull request and fix only what is safe to fix.
```

## 4. Workshop build board

The project-scoped canvas is already implemented in `.github/extensions/workshop-build-board/`.
Ask: "Show the workshop build board." Reload project extensions if discovery has not picked it up.
The Copilot canvas SDK is **Preview**; it is separate from Azure or Foundry agent infrastructure.

The board is a responsive single-page application (SPA) with a module route, a target architecture overview,
participant guides, the build runbook, verified sources and open decisions. There are no status-entry forms,
personal completion checkboxes or participant accounts. Professionals follow the instructions and run the
module's validation themselves.

The **EN / FR** switch changes both the interface and authored learning content. `?lang=fr#/module/04` is a
French deep link. Language selection is URL state, not participant tracking. A future missing `README.fr.md`
is reported explicitly, with the original English participant guide available separately.
Repository evidence and open decisions remain explicitly labeled original English text.
The **Understand governance**, **Your Azure context**, and **Diagrams and icons** pages contain deep dives,
tenant/subscription selection, cost/permission boundaries and diagram provenance. Each module shows a planned
EN/FR diagram and a next step; 06 offers cleanup 99 with 07 as an optional branch.
See [the French runbook](fr/COPILOT-RUNBOOK.md) for French session prompts.

Context selectors are preparation helpers, not the module 00 preflight. They verify the participant-selected
tenant/subscription using Azure CLI, then set Terraform context in the current shell only. They never create
cloud resources, assign roles or save IDs into the board. Read [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md) before running them.

Hash routes such as `#/module/00` support direct links and browser back/forward navigation.
The board reads a published module's README when it exists. Otherwise it shows a clearly labeled design outline
from `docs/BUILD-PLAN.md`; an outline is not a runnable participant procedure. No module is currently implemented.
Use **Refresh content** after changing repository documents. Repository status and evidence are available in
an optional disclosure, separate from the participant instructions.

The interface never writes progress or executes commands. Agent actions still update only `docs/PROGRESS.md`;
no separate progress database exists. Conflicting agent updates are refused rather than overwriting a newer version.
Evidence and notes must be single-line Markdown table cells without pipe characters.

### Practice with your deployed server

Open **Try your MCP** (`#/practice`) for Codespaces setup, local VS Code launch, and client connection help.
Or ask Copilot: "Open the workshop build board on its practice view."
Enter only an HTTPS endpoint with no credentials, query or fragment. This field is held in page memory only:
the board never sends it to its server, saves it or makes an MCP request with it.
Copy buttons prepare commands, a URL-only VS Code user configuration and starter prompts; they do not run them.
If clipboard access is blocked by the host, select the text and copy it manually.

Inspector launch commands pin the registry-available 2.9.0, keep credentials in memory, disable token
persistence, negotiate the protocol era and bind to loopback. The GitHub 2.11.0 source candidate was not yet
available from the configured npm registry during launch testing. Commands fail if the pinned package is
unavailable; they do not change the registry or silently fall back.
Keep its authentication enabled, use its printed private local URL, and do not export credential-bearing headers
or transcripts. Entra requires a separately approved public client, exact redirect URI and delegated scopes;
VS Code preauthorization is not Inspector preauthorization. Never add a client secret.

The Codespaces link opens a creation flow for the published repository. It does not include unpushed local
changes or create a machine automatically. Review costs before creating it. Until module 00 publishes its
dev container, no preconfigured or validated workshop environment is promised. For authenticated Inspector
tests, prefer local desktop or private local forwarding through VS Code desktop, not public browser ports.

Every module has a **Need a hint?** disclosure explaining what its boundary proves and what to check next.
Module validation commands appear only when both the README and the script exist. Running them remains an
explicit participant action with the approvals required by the module. No live test result is inferred from
a connected badge, generated command or example. See [PRACTICE.md](PRACTICE.md) for the full scenarios.

Agent capabilities:

| Capability | Input and result |
|---|---|
| `get_progress` | Returns modules, open decisions, and the current revision. |
| `set_module_status` | Requires module, status, evidence and revision from the last read. Optional notes; validated also requires a real validation date. |
| `list_open_decisions` | Returns the Open decisions section. |

Mark a module `validated` only after its actual validation script passes in the required fresh-subscription
sequence. The board stores evidence; it neither runs tests nor certifies that evidence.
Do not enter confidential text, tokens, prompts, tool arguments/results, or live account identifiers.

To check the board locally without the Copilot runtime:

```powershell
node --test .github\extensions\workshop-build-board\progress.test.mjs
```

### Re-create the board in a different project

In a session, type `/create-canvas`, choose the **project** scope (the canvas is saved in `.github/extensions/`
and versioned with the repository), then paste:

```text
Create a canvas named "Workshop build board". Build a responsive, read-only SPA with hash-route module
navigation, participant instructions from each module README, an overview, verified sources and open decisions.
Remove progress-entry forms. For an unpublished module, show a clearly labeled outline from docs/BUILD-PLAN.md
instead of inventing runnable steps. Show repository status and evidence in a secondary disclosure.
Add a Try your MCP page with Codespaces setup and local launch links, endpoint-only in-memory command preparation,
safe Inspector launch commands and copyable prompts. Calls run in the official client, never through the board.
Read per-module hints from docs/PRACTICE.md. Offer validators only when the README and script actually exist.
Give the agent three capabilities: get_progress, set_module_status(module, status, evidence) and list_open_decisions.
docs/PROGRESS.md stays the only progress source of truth. Only agent actions update it; browsing is read-only.
```

Then ask in any session: "Show the workshop build board and start the next module."

## 5. Before the workshop day

- Dry run: modules 00 to 06 end to end on a fresh subscription with a new prefix, then module 99.
- Record a short video of each level as a fallback.
- Check the cost of the dry run, then clean up.

## Sources

[Build plan](BUILD-PLAN.md), [source register](sources.md), and
[VS Code MCP configuration](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/mcp-servers).
Last verified: 2026-10-14.
'@

$files['docs/DEPLOYMENT.md'] = @'
# Your tenant. Your subscription. Your decision.

The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server belongs in your approved **non-production** environment.
This page prepares a deployment context. It is not a deployment procedure, and it does not certify your permissions.
Start by [choosing dev container or local tools](GETTING-STARTED.md).
Module 00's read-only preflight, validator and identity request are available.
Terraform contains the resource-group foundation and an optional public App Service; no MCP runtime is implemented.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> A Microsoft Entra tenant holds identities, applications and consent. An Azure subscription holds billed resources.
> A resource group is the workshop's resource boundary. These are different scopes and different administrative responsibilities.

## 1. Agree the boundary before signing in

Choose an approved test tenant and an Azure subscription associated with that directory.
Confirm your participant prefix, resource group, owner, expiry, region, budget and cleanup window.
Do not use production data, another person's session, or an employer's unapproved environment.
Keep tenant/subscription IDs and account details in your private terminal, not this board or the repository.

| Responsibility | Required authority | Not implied |
|---|---|---|
| Deploy workload | Approved resource operations on the workshop resource group | Contributor does not grant role-assignment authority. |
| Configure identities | An identity administrator approves exact apps, app roles, test users and consent | An Azure subscription role does not grant Entra administration. |
| Assign resource access | Separately authorized role assignments at the exact target scope | No subscription-wide Owner request by default. |
| Use a client | Approved public client ID, exact callback and scopes | VS Code preauthorization does not authorize Inspector. |
| Reuse existing IDs | Owner supplies approved identifiers and ownership boundaries | Do not modify or destroy those pre-existing identities. |

## 2. Select and verify in your own terminal

Review the context selector before running it. It uses interactive Azure CLI sign-in, selects your subscription,
then checks both IDs and the subscription state. It never calls a resource-creation or role-assignment command.
It rejects conflicting credential overrides instead of silently mixing authentication flows.
The confirmation is your assertion of approval, not an automatic production-environment detector.

### PowerShell 7

From your existing checkout in a PowerShell terminal:

```powershell
& .\scripts\select-azure-context.ps1
```

Use `&` in the current PowerShell process, **not** a new `pwsh -File` process.
The selector sets `ARM_TENANT_ID` and `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` only in that process.
It uses `az login --tenant`, `az account set --subscription` and `az account show`.
Follow your organization's browser and multifactor sign-in policy. Do not use a password or client secret.

### bash

```bash
source ./scripts/select-azure-context.sh en
```

Source the script in the same bash terminal. Executing it as `bash script.sh` is refused because environment
variables would disappear with the child process. Authentication and context checks are equivalent to PowerShell.

> [!WARNING]
> A failed check is a stop, not permission to continue with the previous default subscription.
> Re-select the approved context before any Terraform command. Never paste `az account get-access-token` output here.
> These helpers support Azure public cloud. A sovereign-cloud workshop requires a separately reviewed configuration.

## 3. Review the real module before a paid deployment

1. Complete module 00's published prerequisite/permission checks, then module 01's local MCP exercise.
   Context selection alone does not validate either module.
2. Prepare the private participant file using the start guide. Keep its random suffix and the same Terraform state.
   `infra/workload` manages a dedicated group or reads an approved existing group; the public portal is an explicit paid opt-in.
   Neither creates an MCP server.
   Wait for each runtime module's real guide, validation and cleanup. There is no enabled Deploy button in the board.
3. In the same terminal, review the actual `infra/workload` provider configuration and its subscription/tenant.
   Check AzureRM and AzAPI target the selected subscription, and AzureAD targets the approved tenant.
   An explicit provider setting or alias can override environment defaults; inspect every provider and the plan.
4. The identity administrator reviews `infra/entra` separately. Read existing IDs when creation is skipped.
   Use managed identities and federated identity credentials, never a client secret.
5. Review regional availability, quota, organization policy, tags and cost estimate. Standard v2 API Management
   is provisioned capacity. Private endpoints, storage, telemetry, API Center and optional agents can also cost money.
   A randomized group or service name avoids many collisions but does not reset shared subscription/regional quotas.
6. Read the Terraform plan's exact resources and role scopes. Apply only after explicit approval in the deployment
   session. Recheck context if you change terminals, subscriptions, provider configuration or credentials.
7. Run the module's real validator, retain only approved metadata/PASS lines, and do not proceed until it passes.

## 4. Call the server you actually deployed

Use the endpoint from **your deployment outputs**. Module 01 uses local standard input/output (stdio), not HTTPS.
Module 02 is a short-lived system-key demonstration. Module 03 adds the assigned-user identity boundary.
From module 04 onward, only the governed API Management URL is a client endpoint; the Function is private.

Sign in to the correct tenant with the approved client, exact callback and scopes. Use Alice/Bob/Eve only as
synthetic persona labels for dedicated test identities. Never enter tokens, keys or account credentials in the board.
Use **Try your MCP** to prepare client commands, then make and inspect actual calls in the official client.
An HTTP 200 can contain an MCP tool error. Each synthetic write requires an explicit role and individual approval.

> [!TIP]
> Predict the result before calling: missing token means 401; Bob's gateway write means 403; a burst means 429.
> For downstream delegation, inspect the MCP result as well as HTTP. A denial can be the correct demonstration.

## 5. End the cost and preserve shared objects

Follow module 99 after the paid stages; module 07 is optional. Review workload teardown before identity cleanup.
Only workshop-created resources, assignments and test identities may be removed, after explicit approval.
Preserve passed-in identities and shared infrastructure. Check tagged resources, private networking, inventory,
retention and soft-deletion behavior. Stop Inspector and unused codespaces too.

## Sources

- [Azure CLI subscription and tenant selection](https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/manage-azure-subscriptions-azure-cli).
- [Azure CLI interactive sign-in](https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/authenticate-azure-cli-interactively).
- [Azure role assignment authority](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/role-based-access-control/role-assignments-cli).
- [AzureRM authentication and provider configuration](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/azurerm/5.9.0/docs).
- [Build plan](BUILD-PLAN.md), [practice guide](PRACTICE.md) and [source assessment](sources.md).

Last verified: 2026-10-14. No subscription was accessed or resource deployed to write this guidance.
'@

$files['docs/fr/BUILD-PLAN.md'] = @'
# Le parcours de l'atelier

Ce parcours présente ce que vous apprendrez à chaque étape. Suivez les modules dans l'ordre.
Le module 00 est disponible ; sa validation dans Azure reste à faire. Les autres modules sont encore prévus.
N'utilisez pas ces résumés comme procédure de déploiement : suivez le guide du module lorsqu'il est publié.
Le [plan technique en anglais](../BUILD-PLAN.md) s'adresse aux personnes qui construisent l'atelier.

## 00 - Prérequis et environnement

**Objectif :** vérifier vos outils et préparer un environnement de test autorisé.
**À vérifier :** les contrôles réussissent et l'administrateur comprend la demande de configuration Entra.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : ce qui est disponible et ce qui reste à valider</summary>

Le guide bilingue, les scripts de vérification en lecture seule et la demande Entra sont disponibles.
Les versions des outils sont fixées et chaque participant conserve ses paramètres privés.
Terraform prépare le groupe et un portail App Service B1 facultatif, payant et désactivé par défaut.
Les tests locaux ne remplacent ni un déploiement dans Azure ni un démarrage du conteneur.
Aucun serveur MCP ni code Terraform Entra n'est encore implémenté.

</details>

## 01 - Découvrir MCP sans Azure

**Objectif :** afficher les outils d'un serveur local, puis en exécuter un avec des données fictives.
**À vérifier :** une exécution réelle et une confirmation manuelle disponible dans le client.
Aucun coût Azure ni modèle d'IA n'est nécessaire pour les tests de protocole avec Inspector.
Le serveur C# utilise ses entrées et sorties standard (stdio), pas une adresse HTTPS.

## 02 - Serveur par clé, niveau 0

**Objectif :** comprendre qu'une clé partagée protège l'accès sans identifier chaque utilisateur.
**À vérifier :** HTTP 401 sans clé ; HTTP 200 et réussite de l'outil avec la bonne clé.
Cette étape payante reste courte et ne représente pas la protection finale.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : services et protection de la clé</summary>

Terraform préparera Azure Functions Flex Consumption, le stockage, la télémétrie et une identité managée.
Les outils utiliseront des données fictives ; `create_ticket` ajoutera un message à une file de stockage.
Les accès liés à l'identité resteront simulés jusqu'au module 05.
Le point d'entrée gardera le niveau d'autorisation `System`, jamais un accès anonyme.

</details>

## 03 - Identité Microsoft Entra, niveau 1

**Objectif :** autoriser les utilisateurs prévus, plutôt que toute personne possédant une clé.
**À vérifier :** Alice et Bob peuvent se connecter, pas Eve. Un jeton absent ou destiné à un autre service est refusé.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : application, autorisations et rôles</summary>

L'application MCP aura sa propre audience, l'autorisation déléguée `user_impersonation` et les rôles
Tools.Read, Tools.Write et Tools.Execute.All. Les utilisateurs devront être affectés à l'application.
L'authentification intégrée et les clients préautorisés prépareront la connexion sans secret client.
Les métadonnées de ressource protégée (PRM) sont en **Preview**.
Les tests sans jeton ou avec une mauvaise audience doivent renvoyer HTTP 401.

</details>

## 04 - API Management comme entrée unique, niveau 2

**Objectif :** imposer le passage par la passerelle API Management.
**À vérifier :** Bob ne peut pas créer de ticket, Alice le peut après approbation.
Un nombre excessif d'appels est limité et l'accès direct à la Function est refusé.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : réseau privé et contrôles de la passerelle</summary>

API Management (APIM) Standard v2 utilisera l'API de gestion MCP `2025-09-01-preview`, en **Preview**.
La passerelle vérifiera les jetons Entra et le rôle requis par outil. Elle limitera le débit et filtrera les adresses IP.
L'intégration au réseau sortant, un point de terminaison privé et la résolution privée des noms (DNS)
permettront de joindre la Function sans accès public.
APIM et Functions protégeront le même service MCP. Les journaux ne contiendront aucun corps de requête ou de réponse.
Les résultats attendus sont HTTP 403 pour Bob, HTTP 200 et réussite pour Alice, HTTP 429 au-delà du débit autorisé.
L'option 04b REST vers MCP ne remplace pas le serveur principal.

</details>

## 05 - On-Behalf-Of et accès applicatif, niveau 3

**Objectif :** comparer les droits de l'utilisateur et ceux de l'application.
**À vérifier :** la lecture au nom de l'utilisateur réussit pour Alice et échoue pour Bob ;
la lecture avec les droits de l'application donne le même résultat aux deux.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : délégation et identité managée</summary>

`whoami` appellera Microsoft Graph et `read_report_as_user` appellera Azure Storage avec On-Behalf-Of (OBO).
`read_report_as_app` utilisera une identité managée. Aucun jeton MCP ne sera transmis aux API cibles.
Les identités managées et la fédération remplacent les secrets clients.
Examinez le code HTTP et l'erreur MCP : un refus du stockage peut être contenu dans une réponse HTTP 200.

</details>

## 06 - Observation et inventaire, niveau 4

**Objectif :** savoir qui a utilisé quel outil et avec quel résultat, sans enregistrer les données échangées.
**À vérifier :** les journaux ne contiennent ni jetons, ni consignes, ni paramètres ou résultats d'outils.
Azure API Center répertorie l'adresse de la passerelle ; il ne relaie pas les appels et ne donne aucun droit.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : analyser les métadonnées</summary>

Des requêtes Kusto Query Language (KQL) ou un classeur d'analyse montreront le nom de l'outil,
l'identifiant de l'appelant authentifié, le statut, la durée et l'identifiant de corrélation.
L'inventaire Microsoft 365 est facultatif, en **Preview**, et réservé à l'animateur.
L'intégration [Agent 365 et API Management (Preview)](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/agent-365)
reste une démonstration prévue, non implémentée. API Management v2 permet l'inventaire et le blocage du serveur ;
le contrôle par outil exige AI Gateway (Preview) et une liste statique d'outils autorisés.
Le consentement administrateur à l'échelle du tenant n'est jamais une étape participant.
Les limites et les résultats attendus figurent dans la [fiche de gouvernance](LEARNING-GUIDE.md).

</details>

## 07 - Clients supplémentaires, facultatif

**Objectif :** utiliser le même serveur depuis un client Foundry ou Copilot Studio officiellement pris en charge,
sans secret. Vérifiez les licences, l'identité utilisée et les fonctions en **Preview**.
L'authentification OAuth manuelle Copilot Studio exigeant un secret reste reportée.
Cette démonstration de l'animateur ne doit pas retarder le nettoyage.

## 99 - Nettoyage

**Objectif :** supprimer les ressources créées pour l'atelier, puis les identités et rôles créés pour les tests.
Examinez le plan de suppression et faites-le approuver. Préservez les ressources partagées et les comptes existants.
**À vérifier :** aucune ressource de l'atelier ne reste facturée ; les rôles, identités et durées de conservation sont vérifiés.
Arrêtez aussi Inspector et les codespaces inutilisés.

## Sources

[Plan technique anglais](../BUILD-PLAN.md), [gouvernance](LEARNING-GUIDE.md), [registre](REFERENCES.md).
Dernière vérification : 2026-10-14.
'@

$files['docs/fr/COPILOT-RUNBOOK.md'] = @'
# Préparer l'atelier avec l'application GitHub Copilot

Ce guide s'adresse aux personnes qui construisent l'atelier, pas aux participants qui suivent les exercices.
**Un module = une session = une branche = une pull request (PR).** Fusionnez avant le module suivant.
Le module 00 est implémenté localement ; aucun module n'est encore validé dans Azure.

Les participants peuvent travailler sans ce processus de construction :
[choisir leur environnement](GETTING-STARTED.md), VS Code avec un conteneur de développement ou un terminal local,
vérifier les outils puis préparer leurs paramètres privés et le groupe Terraform.
Le tableau s'ouvre par défaut sur `#/start`. Choisir un environnement ne valide aucun module.

## Avant de commencer

Installez et connectez GitHub Copilot app selon la politique de votre organisation.
Les versions candidates de Git, GitHub CLI, Azure CLI, Terraform, PowerShell 7, Node.js, .NET et Functions Core Tools
sont décrites dans les [références](REFERENCES.md). Le module 00 fixe leurs versions et prévoit les contrôles.
Choisissez votre [tenant et abonnement de test](DEPLOYMENT.md). L'administrateur d'identité approuve séparément
applications, consentements et comptes de test Alice, Bob et Eve. Aucun identifiant réel dans le dépôt.

Le serveur MCP Microsoft Learn public est configuré dans `.mcp.json` et `.vscode/mcp.json` pour compatibilité :
`https://learn.microsoft.com/api/mcp`, HTTP, sans authentification.
Pour un autre client, ajoutez-le via Customize ou `/mcp add`, nom `microsoft-learn`. Évitez les doublons.

## Démarrer depuis les bons fichiers

Si vous avez déjà le dépôt, ouvrez ce dossier en session **locale**. N'y relancez pas le bootstrap.
Pour une destination neuve et vide seulement :

```powershell
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\setup-workshop-mcp.ps1
```

Le script nécessite Git et GitHub CLI sauf `-SkipGitHub`. Si nécessaire, installez ces outils via les commandes
winget indiquées par le script, puis rouvrez le terminal. L'identité Git doit être configurée.
La publication utilise une identité noreply locale au dépôt, sans modifier l'identité Git globale.
Le bootstrap refuse un dossier non vide et un dépôt distant existant : ne videz pas un dossier pour contourner ce contrôle.
Options : `-Visibility private`, `-SkipGitHub`, `-OpenVSCode`, `-Path`, `-RepoName`.
Il ne crée ni ne modifie de ressource Azure ou Entra. Une publication nouvelle nécessite votre approbation.

## Modes et responsabilités

| Mode | Usage |
|---|---|
| Plan | Lire les sources et proposer avant de coder. |
| Interactive, session locale | Tout accès ou changement Azure/Entra, avec votre contexte Azure CLI. |
| Autopilot | Documentation seulement, éventuellement en parallèle. |

`docs/BUILD-PLAN.md` est le plan technique canonique en anglais. `docs/PROGRESS.md` reste la seule source de statut.
L'agent suit `.github/skills/workshop-module-builder/SKILL.md` et `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.
Il doit présenter le plan Terraform, les objets Entra, rôles, coûts, Preview et nettoyage puis attendre votre
approbation explicite avant tout changement cloud.

## Consignes à copier dans Copilot

Les consignes ci-dessous sont à copier dans une session Copilot. Le tableau ne les exécute pas.

<details>
<summary>Pour aller plus loin : consignes de construction P1 à P9 et documentation</summary>

### P1 - Plan global, sans code

```text
Lis .github/copilot-instructions.md, docs/BUILD-PLAN.md, docs/PROGRESS.md puis les sources officielles.
Propose l'arborescence complète, les fichiers par module et les choix réseau, providers Terraform, région,
permissions Entra, Preview et coûts. Ne code pas. Consigne les décisions à résoudre dans docs/PROGRESS.md.
```

### P2 - Modules 00 puis 01, sessions séparées

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 00 : devcontainer, précontrôle et demande d'identité précise.
Arrête après réussite de sa validation. Mets à jour docs/PROGRESS.md et ouvre la PR.
Après fusion, démarre une autre session et branche pour le module 01 ; arrête après réussite du validateur.
```

### P3 - Module 02, serveur par clé

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 02, préfixe participant p00 et contexte de test approuvé.
Montre le plan Terraform et les coûts avant application. Attends mon approbation.
Utilise la clé système MCP et l'autorisation System, jamais un endpoint anonyme. Valide puis ouvre la PR.
```

### P4 - Module 03, Microsoft Entra

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 03. Liste exactement objets Entra, permissions et consentements
de infra/entra ; applique seulement après approbation. Active l'authentification intégrée et les métadonnées PRM
(Preview), sans secret client. Prouve les résultats attendus pour Alice, Bob et Eve.
```

### P5 - Module 04, point d'entrée imposé

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 04. API Management est le seul point d'entrée.
Prouve avec validate.ps1 : écriture de Bob 403, Alice 200 et succès MCP, seuil de débit 429,
Function directe refusée et journaux sans corps. Présente le plan et attends mon accord avant modification.
```

### P6 - Module 05, délégation et app-only

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 05. Compare OBO et identité managée avec Alice et Bob.
Valide les quatre cas, avec statut HTTP et indicateur d'erreur MCP. Aucun secret, transfert de jeton MCP
aux API cibles ni repli sur l'identité développeur.
```

### P7 - Module 06, observation et inventaire

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 06 : KQL ou workbook limité à outil, ID objet de l'appelant
authentifié, statut, latence et corrélation. Inscris l'URL gouvernée dans API Center. Zéro octet de corps.
Toute création ou modification nécessite mon accord préalable.
```

### P8 - Module 99, nettoyage

```text
Utilise workshop-module-builder pour le module 99. Présente le vrai plan de destruction et attends mon accord.
Supprime les ressources Azure avant les seuls objets Entra créés pour l'atelier. Préserve les identités fournies et les ressources
partagées. Vérifie inventaire, rôles, réseau privé, rétention et coûts restants.
```

### PD - Documentation en parallèle

```text
Écris les explications et le guide de l'animateur à partir du plan et des modules réellement validés.
Synchronise les explications françaises et les schémas éditables. Ne modifie ni code de module ni infrastructure.
Mets à jour les sources et ouvre une PR.
```

### P9 - Revue finale

```text
Vérifie les neuf invariants, l'absence de contenu privé, les liens, les mentions Preview et le nettoyage
sur un abonnement neuf. Liste les écarts dans une PR. Ne corrige que ce qui est sûr et approuvé.
```

</details>

Le module 07 est une démonstration facultative de l'animateur ; il ne doit pas retarder le nettoyage.
Avant l'atelier, répétez les modules 00 à 06 puis 99 sur un abonnement de test neuf.
Vérifiez les coûts et préparez des vidéos de secours.

## Utiliser le tableau

Demandez « Affiche le Workshop build board ». Le SDK canvas Copilot est en **Preview**, indépendant d'un agent Foundry.
Choisissez EN ou FR. `?lang=fr#/module/04` ouvre directement le module 04 en français.
L'URL conserve le choix de langue ; aucun compte ni suivi personnel n'est créé.
Les pages incluent schémas, gouvernance, références, contexte Azure et espace de test.

Le tableau ne change aucun statut ni aucune ressource et n'exécute aucune commande. Il affiche le guide publié lorsqu'il existe ;
sinon, une conception explicitement non exécutable. Si un futur README français manque, il le dit et permet de
lire l'original anglais sans prétendre l'avoir traduit.
Les décisions et preuves de construction conservent leur texte original anglais, clairement signalé.

Dans **Tester votre serveur MCP**, seule une adresse HTTPS sans clé ni paramètre reste en mémoire de page.
Les copies de commandes, configurations et prompts ne font aucun appel réseau MCP.
Codespaces ouvre le choix de création de la branche publiée, pas une machine prête ni les modifications non poussées.
Inspector reste privé, authentifié et sans stockage de jetons. Chaque client Entra demande sa propre approbation.

Les actions agent restent `get_progress`, `set_module_status` et `list_open_decisions`.
L'écriture de statut exige la révision courante. `validated` exige date et vraie preuve du validateur.
Le tableau ne certifie pas cette preuve. N'y mettez ni jetons, consignes, paramètres ou résultats d'outils,
ni données réelles de comptes.

## Sources

[Plan technique original](../BUILD-PLAN.md), [registre](REFERENCES.md), [pratique](PRACTICE.md),
[configuration VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/mcp-servers).
Dernière vérification : 2026-10-14.
